  ("IDEA-APPIMAGE-XDG-UTILS" . (
    (priority . 4.0)
    (description . "PARTIAL 2026-08-18. Installed xdg-utils natively (sudo apt-get install -y xdg-utils) — /usr/bin/xdg-open now exists, confirmed via `which xdg-open`. Tried adding \"xdg-utils\" to manifest.scm's Guix package list first: broke pkg-config resolution for gio-sys (profile rebuild left pkg-config unfindable) — reverted, documented as a known-bad approach in manifest.scm's comments, native apt install is the working path (matches how Bun/build-essential/rustup are handled). NOT closing: re-running bun run tauri build afterward hit a *different*, pre-existing pkg-config failure (gio-sys/gobject-sys can't find pkg-config even with the reverted manifest) that also blocked the deb/rpm steps this time, unrelated to xdg-utils — see new IDEA-PKG-CONFIG-REGRESSION. Re-verify AppImage specifically once that's fixed.")
    (done . ())
  ))
  ("IDEA-PKG-CONFIG-REGRESSION" . (
    (priority . 9.5)
    (description . "ROOT-CAUSED 2026-08-18. bun run tauri build (and plain cargo check) intermittently fail with 'The pkg-config command could not be found' inside gio-sys/gobject-sys build scripts, specifically when entering the environment via `guix time-machine -C ../my-lisp/channels.scm -- shell -m manifest.scm` — repro'd 3x. Switching to plain `guix shell -m manifest.scm` (same manifest.scm, no time-machine channel pin) made cargo check pass clean on the first try, compiling the new ecosystem/repo_graph.rs module with zero errors. Root cause: guix time-machine re-fetches/re-authenticates the pinned channel (visible 'channel guix lacks an introduction' warning) and appears to rebuild a working profile whose pkg-config isn't correctly wired into cargo build-script subprocess PATH — a time-machine-specific profile-construction bug, not a missing package (pkg-config IS present and `which pkg-config` resolves fine in both cases). Workaround: prefer plain `guix shell -m manifest.scm` for iterative cargo check/build; reserve time-machine for when the rustc-too-old blocker actually requires the pinned rust 1.93.0 (see AGENTS.md's rustc-too-old section) — document this distinction in AGENTS.md.")
    (done . t)
  ))
